<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Anhalt-Dessau</h1><p class="st-meta">1603 CE – 1863 CE · capital Dessau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Anhalt principality, famed for the Old Dessauer's drill reforms and the Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm of the Enlightenment.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> was created in the 1603 partition of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a>, with Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-prince-of-anhalt-dessau">John George I</a> receiving Dessau on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulde">Mulde</a> near its junction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. The line was Calvinist and closely tied to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Electorate of Brandenburg</a> by marriage.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-prince-of-anhalt-dessau">Leopold I</a>, the Old Dessauer, was a Prussian field marshal who introduced the iron ramrod and cadenced marching. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-frederick-franz">Leopold III Friedrich Franz</a> was a model <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ruler who laid out the landscape gardens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau-worlitz-garden-realm">Wörlitz</a>, built schools and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philanthropinum">Philanthropinum</a>, a progressive school, in 1774. The principality became a duchy in 1807.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> inherited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-kothen">Anhalt-Köthen</a> in 1847 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-bernburg">Anhalt-Bernburg</a> in 1863, after which Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iv-duke-of-anhalt">Leopold IV Friedrich</a> ruled a united <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a> from Dessau. The Garden Realm is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-anhalt-dessau.jpg" alt="Principality of Anhalt-Dessau" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Anhalt_Duchies.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1603 CE – 1863 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dessau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John George I, Leopold I, Leopold III Friedrich Franz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Anhalt-Dessau, Anhalt-Dessau</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-dessau" aria-label="Principality of Anhalt-Dessau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4956 385 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Anhalt-Dessau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5276 641L5276 641M5276 641L5229 694M5276 641L5199 691M5276 641L5276 641M5276 641L5270 644"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5276 641h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5276 641h0M5270 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5276 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5276 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dessau. <a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-dessau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
